Why does it flake, and what fixes it?$qz$, 'multiple_choice', 5 from newq returning id),
a5 as (insert into public.quiz_answers (question_id, answer_text, is_correct) select q5.id, v.t, v.c from q5, (values ($qz$It flakes because `textContent` is deprecated; switch to `innerText`$qz$, false), ($qz$It reads the status immediately after the click without waiting for it to change; use `await expect(page.getByTestId('status')).toHaveText('Done')` which retries$qz$, true), ($qz$It flakes because the button name is wrong; rename it$qz$, false), ($qz$It flakes because `expect` cannot compare strings$qz$, false)) as v(t, c) returning question_id),
q6 as (insert into public.quiz_questions (quiz_id, question_text, question_type, sort_order) select id, $qz$Why is `page.locator('button').nth(4)` a fragile locator in a test that keeps breaking after UI tweaks?$qz$, 'multiple_choice', 6 from newq returning id),
a6 as (insert into public.quiz_answers (question_id, answer_text, is_correct) select q6.id, v.t, v.c from q6, (values ($qz$Because `nth` is not supported$qz$, false), ($qz$Because it depends on the exact number and order of buttons, so adding or reordering any button silently changes what it selects$qz$, true), ($qz$Because it always selects the wrong element by design$qz$, false), ($qz$Because indexing is slower than other locators$qz$, false)) as v(t, c) returning question_id),
q7 as (insert into public.quiz_questions (quiz_id, question_text, question_type, sort_order) select id, $qz$A suite shares one logged-in account across parallel workers, and tests intermittently corrupt each other's data. What is the most robust fix?$qz$, 'multiple_choice', 7 from newq returning id),
a7 as (insert into public.quiz_answers (question_id, answer_text, is_correct) select q7.id, v.t, v.c from q7, (values ($qz$Run everything on a single worker forever$qz$, false), ($qz$Isolate test data so each test (or worker) uses its own account or fixtures, removing the shared-state dependency$qz$, true), ($qz$Add random sleeps to space the tests out$qz$, false), ($qz$Increase the number of workers so there is less contention$qz$, false)) as v(t, c) returning question_id),
q8 as (insert into public.quiz_questions (quiz_id, question_text, question_type, sort_order) select id, $qz$What is the main advantage of Playwright's trace viewer when debugging a flaky failure?$qz$, 'multiple_choice', 8 from newq returning id),
a8 as (insert into public.quiz_answers (question_id, answer_text, is_correct) select q8.id, v.t, v.c from q8, (values ($qz$It rewrites the failing test automatically$qz$, false), ($qz$It captures a timeline of actions, DOM snapshots, and network activity so you can see the exact state when the test failed$qz$, true), ($qz$It disables flakiness by removing timing from the run$qz$, false), ($qz$It only records the final screenshot and nothing else$qz$, false)) as v(t, c) returning question_id),
q9 as (insert into public.quiz_questions (quiz_id, question_text, question_type, sort_order) select id, $qz$A test asserts on an animation's end state but occasionally catches it mid-transition. Which approach is most reliable?$qz$, 'multiple_choice', 9 from newq returning id),
a9 as (insert into public.quiz_answers (question_id, answer_text, is_correct) select q9.id, v.t, v.c from q9, (values ($qz$Sleep for exactly the animation's duration in milliseconds$qz$, false), ($qz$Assert on the stable end condition (for example the final text or a settled attribute) with a web-first assertion that retries until it holds$qz$, true), ($qz$Disable all CSS on the page before every test$qz$, false), ($qz$Re-run the test until it happens to pass$qz$, false)) as v(t, c) returning question_id),
q10 as (insert into public.quiz_questions (quiz_id, question_text, question_type, sort_order) select id, $qz$Which statement best captures the right attitude toward the `retries` config option?$qz$, 'multiple_choice', 10 from newq returning id),
a10 as (insert into public.quiz_answers (question_id, answer_text, is_correct) select q10.id, v.t, v.c from q10, (values ($qz$Retries are a permanent cure for flakiness and should be maxed out$qz$, false), ($qz$Retries can reduce noise from genuinely rare flakiness, but a test that regularly needs them signals a real problem to fix, not hide$qz$, true), ($qz$Retries should never be used under any circumstances$qz$, false), ($qz$Retries change the application code to make it faster$qz$, false)) as v(t, c) returning question_id),
q11 as (insert into public.quiz_questions (quiz_id, question_text, question_type, sort_order) select id, $qz$You remove a hard wait and replace it with a web-first assertion, and the suite gets both faster and more stable. Why does the assertion improve speed as well as reliability?$qz$, 'multiple_choice', 11 from newq returning id),
a11 as (insert into public.quiz_answers (question_id, answer_text, is_correct) select q11.id, v.t, v.c from q11, (values ($qz$Because assertions run on a separate thread$qz$, false), ($qz$Because it proceeds the moment the condition is met instead of always waiting a fixed duration, and it keeps retrying until the timeout only if needed$qz$, true), ($qz$Because it skips the check entirely on fast machines$qz$, false), ($qz$Because web-first assertions do not actually wait for anything$qz$, false)) as v(t, c) returning question_id)
